    "question": {
      ":question/id": "22669eb4-c329-56a8-ada8-532ae9df6656",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy+2x{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+2x{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =y+2{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =y+2{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2x{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =x+4xy+0 \\\\  & =x+4xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+2x{y}^{2}+4{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+12{z}^{2} \\\\  & =12{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=(y+2{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+4xy)\\,j+12{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+4xy)\\,j+12{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ x+4xy=0 \\\\ 12{z}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(1+2y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=- \\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4x(- \\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,- \\frac{1}{2},0).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
