  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124445,
      "id": "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340",
      "title": "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "34215f1a-c5c8-5fd7-985b-86c43e205b9e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{14}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{7}{zx}-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇f(x,y,z)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{14}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{7}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{7}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14z+7y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{14}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{7}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14+2x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{14}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{7}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{7}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{14z+7y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{14+2x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{7+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{14z+7y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{14+2x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{7+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{14z+7y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{14+2x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{7+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-7.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{7+{z}^{2}(-7)}{{z}^{2}(-7)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-7$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{14z+7y}{(-7)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ 14z+7y & =0 \\\\ y & =-2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-2$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=2.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-7,-2,1)$ and $(-7,2,-1).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "275cc0d4-6206-56cc-b7d0-a4e6a6e7b312",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
